Issues:
1. `smoothShape(outline, 14, true)` — my smoothShape signature is (points, samples, closed) and it does `curve.getSpacedPoints(samples * pts.length)` → 14*29 = 406 points. Then the Shape is built with lineTo through 407 points → ExtrudeGeometry with curveSegments: 6 will tessellate each line segment... For a Shape made of lineTo segments, curveSegments doesn't subdivide lines (each lineTo is one segment). So the outline will have 406 segments → the extrude produces 406*2 wall quads + caps triangulated from 406 points. That's ~2500 triangles. Acceptable. But it's overkill; let me reduce samples to 6 → 174 points. Fine, keep 14 for smoothness; it's a one-time cost.

Hmm, but SplineCurve.getSpacedPoints with closed=true: does SplineCurve handle closed? SplineCurve is a 2D Catmull-Rom curve; `closed` is a property of Curve... In three, `CurvePath`/`Path` have `closed`; the base `Curve` class doesn't have a `closed` property used by getPoint... Actually CatmullRomCurve3 has `closed`. SplineCurve extends Curve and implements getPoint(t) using `points` with the Catmull-Rom spline; it does NOT handle closed (it clamps the index). Setting `curve.closed = true` on a SplineCurve has no effect on getPoint.

Let me check three's SplineCurve implementation:
```js
class SplineCurve extends Curve {
  getPoint(t, optionalTarget = new Vector2()) {
    const point = optionalTarget;
    const points = this.points;
    const p = (points.length - 1) * t;
    const intPoint = Math.floor(p);
    const weight = p - intPoint;
    const p0 = points[intPoint === 0 ? intPoint : intPoint - 1];
    const p1 = points[intPoint];
    const p2 = points[intPoint > points.length - 2 ? points.length - 1 : intPoint + 1];
    const p3 = points[intPoint > points.length - 3 ? points.length - 1 : intPoint + 2];
    ...
  }
}
```
Yes — no closed support. So my `curve.closed = closed` line does nothing. Since I build the outline as a closed list where the last point ≈ the first point (the outline goes nose → tail right side → tail left side → back to the nose), the SplineCurve from t=0 to 1 covers the full loop, and getSpacedPoints' last point = the last point in the list = [1.72, 0] = the first point ✓ (since I include STATIONS[0] at the start and the loop ends by returning to index 0? Let me check: `for (let i = STATIONS.length - 2; i >= 1; i--) outline.push(...)` → the last pushed is i=1 → [1.6, -0.075]. So the outline list ends at (1.6,-0.075), not at the nose tip (1.72, 0). The shape's closePath will connect (1.6,-0.075) back to the start (1.72, 0) with a straight line — a tiny chord at the nose, acceptable. But the spline through the last point won't be smooth into the first.

Better: append the first point at the end of the list so the spline closes smoothly: outline.push([1.72, 0]) → then the last point = the first point, and the shape's closePath is a no-op ✓. But the Catmull-Rom tangents at the seam will be computed from (p_{n-2}, p_{n-1}=first, ...) — the tangent at the start uses points[0], points[1] and the tangent at the end uses the previous points → there'd be a slight kink at the nose, but since both ends are the same point and the neighbors are nearly symmetric (the first two are (1.6,0.075) and the last two are (1.6,-0.075)), the tangents at the seam: for the start, p0 = points[0] (clamped), p1 = points[0], p2 = points[1] → the tangent ∝ (p2-p0)/2 = ((1.6,0.075)-(1.72,0))/2 = (-0.06, 0.0375). For the end, the tangent ∝ (p_last - p_{last-1}) = ((1.72,0)-(1.6,-0.075)) = (0.12, 0.075) → direction (0.6,0.375) vs the start's (-0.6, 0.375) → mirrored, so the closed loop is smooth at the nose ✓ (the tangent directions are consistent around the loop: going up on one side and down on the other, forming a point). 

So append the first station at the end. Let me fix that.

2. `blank.translate(0, -(0.05/2), 0)`: after rotateX(-π/2), the y range is [-bevelThickness, depth+bevelThickness] = [-0.026, 0.076]. Center = 0.025. So translate by -0.025 ✓ = -(0.05/2) ✓. Then y ∈ [-0.051, 0.051]. But DECK_Y = 0.0555 — slightly larger than the actual 0.051. Let me set DECK_Y = 0.051 to match. Actually the deck top after the bevel: the topmost y = depth + bevelThickness - 0.025 = 0.076-0.025 = 0.051 ✓. Set DECK_Y = 0.051.

